      "title": "Kinds of deductibles",
      "narration": "A deductible is the amount the insured pays before the plan pays. Most are calendar-year deductibles that reset each January. A per-cause deductible applies to each separate illness or injury instead. Family plans use an individual deductible for each person and a family deductible, often two or three times the individual amount, after which everyone is covered. A carryover provision lets expenses in the last three months of a year count toward next year’s deductible. A common accident provision applies one deductible when several family members are hurt in the same accident. Preventive services are covered before the deductible.",
