      "title": "Stop-loss and maximums",
      "narration": "A stop-loss provision ends the insured’s coinsurance once out-of-pocket spending reaches a stated limit; after that the plan pays 100 percent of covered charges for the rest of the year. Federal law requires every comprehensive plan to have an annual out-of-pocket maximum, indexed each year, that includes the deductible, coinsurance and copayments but not premiums or balance bills. Older plans also carried lifetime maximum benefits, such as one million dollars; those are now prohibited for essential health benefits. Excepted-benefit policies may still cap benefits, which is another reason they are not substitutes for major medical.",
